                <pb facs="0644" n="572" sameAs="frus:frus1909/pg572"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 10044/169.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Ambassador Leishman to the Secretary of
                    State.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Embassy</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Pera</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1909-04-29"><hi
                                rendition="#italic">April 29, 1909</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Mr. Leishman says now that good order has been restored in capital, troops are
                    being hurried to disturbed provinces, and that the thorough manner in which the
                    reestablished constitutional Government is taking hold of things encourages the
                    belief that the trouble in the Adana and Alexandretta districts will soon
                    disappear. He says martial law will probably be continued for another week or
                    two as a necessary measure, and that trials of offenders are rapidly proceeding
                    and ordinary business is being resumed everywhere. Mr. Leishman adds that the
                    Sultan has been taken to Salonica.</p>
            </div>
